@rem Coding Exam 1 (C): statements, solutions and tests taken verbatim from the exam preparation paper.
@rem Tests that depended on the shared array state of the original main() start from the state at that point.

@task c-leap-year
@title isLeapYear
@points 10
@lesson 01_intro
@statement
Q1: Check if an inputted year is a Leap Year.
A leap year is divisible by 4, but not by 100 unless also divisible by 400.
@includes
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
@stub
bool isLeapYear(int year) {
    // your code here
    return false;
}
@solution
bool isLeapYear(int year) {
    if (year % 400 == 0) {
        return true;
    }
    if (year % 100 == 0) {
        return false;
    }
    if (year % 4 == 0) {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
@tests
printf("--- Q1: Leap Year Test ---\n");
if (!isLeapYear(1700)) printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");
if (isLeapYear(1600))  printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");
if (isLeapYear(2016))  printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");
@expect
--- Q1: Leap Year Test ---
yes
yes
yes
@checklist
- Divisible by 400 is a leap year.
- Divisible by 100 (but not 400) is not a leap year.
- Otherwise divisible by 4 is a leap year.

@task c-reversed-palindrome
@title Reversed and isAPalindrome
@points 20
@lesson 01_intro
@statement
Q2 (helper): Reverse the digits of a number and return the reversed integer.
E.g., 123 -> 321.

Q2: Check Whether a Number is Palindrome or Not.
A palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards (e.g., 121).
@includes
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
@stub
int Reversed(int testNumber) {
    // your code here
    return 0;
}

bool isAPalindrome(int testNumber) {
    // your code here
    return false;
}
@solution
int Reversed(int testNumber) {
    int reversed = 0;
    while (testNumber > 0) {
        int digit = testNumber % 10;        // take the last digit
        reversed = reversed * 10 + digit;   // add it to the end of reversed
        testNumber = testNumber / 10;       // remove the last digit
    }
    return reversed;
}

bool isAPalindrome(int testNumber) {
    if (Reversed(testNumber) == testNumber) {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
@tests
printf("\n--- Q2: Reversed Number Test ---\n");
if (Reversed(123) == 321)  printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");
if (Reversed(1) == 1)      printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");
if (Reversed(1234) != 321) printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");

printf("\n--- Q2: Palindrome Test ---\n");
if (isAPalindrome(1))    printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");
if (isAPalindrome(121))  printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");
if (!isAPalindrome(1213)) printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");
@expect

--- Q2: Reversed Number Test ---
yes
yes
yes

--- Q2: Palindrome Test ---
yes
yes
yes
@checklist
- Reversed builds the number digit by digit: reversed * 10 + digit.
- isAPalindrome compares the number with its reverse.

@task c-prime
@title isAPrimeNumber
@points 10
@lesson 01_intro
@statement
Q3: Check Whether a Number is Prime or Not.
A prime number is only divisible by itself and 1.
@includes
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
@stub
bool isAPrimeNumber(int numbertoTest) {
    // your code here
    return false;
}
@solution
bool isAPrimeNumber(int numbertoTest) {
    if (numbertoTest <= 1) {
        return false;
    }
    // Only check divisors up to the square root.
    // "i <= numbertoTest / i" means the same as "i * i <= numbertoTest"
    // but it never overflows an int (important for 2147483647).
    for (int i = 2; i <= numbertoTest / i; i++) {
        if (numbertoTest % i == 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
@tests
printf("\n--- Q3: Prime Number Test ---\n");
if (isAPrimeNumber(3))          printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");
if (isAPrimeNumber(2147483647)) printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");
if (!isAPrimeNumber(4))         printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");
@expect

--- Q3: Prime Number Test ---
yes
yes
yes
@checklist
- Numbers <= 1 are not prime.
- Only divisors up to the square root are tried.
- Does not overflow for 2147483647 (i <= n / i instead of i * i <= n).

@task c-binary-to-decimal
@title convertBinarytoDecimal
@points 10
@lesson 01_intro
@statement
Q4: Convert Binary Number to Decimal without using the shift operator.
E.g., 110 -> 6.
@includes
#include <stdio.h>
@stub
int convertBinarytoDecimal(int binaryNumber) {
    // your code here
    return 0;
}
@solution
int convertBinarytoDecimal(int binaryNumber) {
    int decimal = 0;
    int power = 1;                          // 1, 2, 4, 8, ...
    while (binaryNumber > 0) {
        int digit = binaryNumber % 10;      // last binary digit
        decimal = decimal + digit * power;
        power = power * 2;
        binaryNumber = binaryNumber / 10;
    }
    return decimal;
}
@tests
printf("\n--- Q4: Binary to Decimal Test ---\n");
if (convertBinarytoDecimal(110) == 6) printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");
if (convertBinarytoDecimal(111) == 7) printf("yes\n"); else printf("no\n");
@expect

--- Q4: Binary to Decimal Test ---
yes
yes
@checklist
- Reads the binary digits from the right with % 10 and / 10.
- Multiplies each digit by the matching power of two without using the shift operator.

@task c-right-triangle
@title drawRightAngledTriangle
@points 10
@lesson 01_intro
@statement
Q6: Output a right-angled triangle of 'A's using 2 nested for loops:
A
AA
AAA
AAAA
@includes
#include <stdio.h>
@stub
void drawRightAngledTriangle() {
    // your code here
}
@solution
void drawRightAngledTriangle() {
    for (int row = 1; row <= 4; row++) {
        for (int col = 1; col <= row; col++) {
